<article class="env-lemma" id="0AY8">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0AY8">Lemma <span data-tag="0AY8">37.53.6</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to S$ be a morphism of schemes. Assume </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$f$ is proper, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$S$ is integral with generic point $\xi $, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$S$ is normal, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$X$ is reduced, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
every generic point of an irreducible component of $X$ maps to $\xi $, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
we have $H^0(X_\xi , \mathcal{O}) = \kappa (\xi )$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Then $f_*\mathcal{O}_ X = \mathcal{O}_ S$ and $f$ has geometrically connected fibres. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Apply Theorem <a href="/tag/03H2" data-tag="03H2">37.53.5</a> to get a factorization $X \to S' \to S$. It is enough to show that $S' = S$. This will follow from Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/0AB1" data-tag="0AB1">29.55.8</a>. Namely, $S'$ is reduced because $X$ is reduced (Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/0AXN" data-tag="0AXN">29.54.8</a>). The morphism $S' \to S$ is integral by the theorem cited above. Every generic point of $S'$ lies over $\xi $ by Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/0AXP" data-tag="0AXP">29.54.9</a> and assumption (5). On the other hand, since $S'$ is the relative spectrum of $f_*\mathcal{O}_ X$ we see that the scheme theoretic fibre $S'_\xi $ is the spectrum of $H^0(X_\xi , \mathcal{O})$ which is equal to $\kappa (\xi )$ by assumption. Hence $S'$ is an integral scheme with function field equal to the function field of $S$. This finishes the proof. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>